  "summary": "irAEGIS and MANTIS predict individual toxicity and survival, while CALM, DiseaseCIO and RNAactDrug 2.0 link omics data to clinical and drug outcomes.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is tying high-dimensional molecular data to clinical endpoints. irAEGIS uses a pathway-guided, interpretable hierarchical model to predict immune-related adverse event risk from pre-treatment scRNA-seq and reports outperforming seven comparators. MANTIS trains a neural network on genome-wide DNA methylation from 2,786 patients to predict time-dependent survival and treatment effects in childhood brain tumors. Resource-scale efforts are more descriptive: CALM integrates single-cell and bulk RNA-seq of AML with clinical annotations, DiseaseCIO links multi-omics to intervention outcomes across 94 disease states, and RNAactDrug 2.0 connects RNA, CNV, mutation, methylation and enhancer features to drug sensitivity. A secondary strand targets the reliability of cancer transcriptome inference: RIFTT and a 569,640-chimeric-RNA atlas with dependency screening address fusion detection, OncoSpot simulates spatial data with known subclones and CNAs for benchmarking, and SpatioCAD and flashscenic target spatially variable gene detection and GRN scalability.",
